<h1>Lander — Orbiter on PHP hosting</h1>
<p class="lead">Lander is the Orbiter admin for ordinary shared hosting: PHP 8.1+ and SQLite, no Node, no build server. Same SQL file (<code>.pod</code> — a plain SQLite database, only renamed), same Station interface. <a href="/lander">Overview page →</a></p>

<h2>When to use it</h2>
<p>Orbiter runs on Node. Many hosts (World4You, Strato, IONOS, cPanel) only offer PHP. Lander gives editors the same admin there, while your Astro site reads published content from a live API.</p>

<h2>What you get</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The Orbiter admin</strong> (Station mode, themes, dock) as static files, backed by a PHP API with the same paths and JSON formats.</li>
  <li><strong>Live read API</strong> — <code>/api/public/&lt;collection&gt;[/&lt;slug&gt;]</code>, CORS for your site's domains and a short cache (10 s by default, <code>public_cache_seconds</code>). Changes appear without a rebuild.</li>
  <li><strong>Markdown import</strong> — <code>.md</code> with frontmatter via Admin → Import or <code>bin/lander import-md</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Media as files</strong> in <code>public/media/</code> (JPG, PNG, WebP, GIF, AVIF, PDF, MP3, M4A, WAV, MP4 and more; real file type checked, PHP execution blocked in that folder). Media fields are resolved to <code>&#123;id, url, alt, type&#125;</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Music and video</strong> — MP3, M4A, AAC, OGG, WAV, FLAC, MP4, WebM and MOV can be uploaded (limit <code>max_upload_mb</code>, 40 for new installs) and are served with range requests. Use a <code>file</code> field and read <code>.url</code> in your site.</li>
  <li><strong>Made for phones and tablets</strong> — dock labels, drawers as panels, list rows with a “⋯” menu and swipes, touch-sized controls, an installable admin (home screen, safe areas). The admin is German by default; change it under Settings → Admin language.</li>
  <li><strong>Preview</strong> — set the meta <code>site.preview</code> (for example <code>&#123;"events": "/events/?vorschau=&#123;slug&#125;"&#125;</code>) and <code>site.url</code>; the editor then shows a <em>Preview</em> button that opens the real page, drafts included while you are signed in.</li>
  <li><strong>Editor role</strong> — editors see a short dock and cannot touch schema, users, build or import (the server answers 403).</li>
  <li><strong>Inbox</strong> for form submissions (server-to-server with a <code>form_token</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Users</strong> — admins add editors and admins in the admin (create, change role, reset password, delete).</li>
  <li><strong>Install and update</strong> — an installer that detects subfolder or domain root, weekly automatic backups, image variants (<code>?w=800&amp;fmt=webp</code>), and a signed one-click update from the admin.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Install</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Upload the folder and set the <strong>document root to <code>public/</code></strong>, ideally on its own subdomain (the admin uses absolute paths).</li>
  <li>Create <code>lander.config.php</code> (<code>public_origins</code> = your site's domain, <code>media_url</code> = full URL).</li>
  <li>Make <code>data/</code> and <code>public/media/</code> writable for PHP.</li>
  <li>Create a user with <code>php bin/lander user &lt;name&gt; admin</code> (SSH), or create it locally and upload the SQL file <code>data/content.pod</code>.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Speed: versioned assets</h2>
<p>The admin pages are sent through PHP, which adds <code>?v=…</code> to scripts and styles; the version changes with every file change. With <code>mod_rewrite</code> and <code>mod_headers</code> those files may stay in the browser cache for a year (<code>public/.htaccess</code> ships the rule). Without them everything still works, the browser just asks again each time.</p>

<h2>Connect your site</h2>
<pre>const events = await fetch('https://redaktion.example.at/api/public/events').then(r =&gt; r.json());</pre>
<p>At build time (SEO) query the same URL in the Astro frontmatter; at runtime the page is always current.</p>

<h2>Backup and restore</h2>
<p>Settings → Pod → <strong>Backup &amp; restore</strong> (admins only). Download the <strong>SQL file</strong> (<code>.pod</code>, small and fast) or a <strong>ZIP with media</strong>. <strong>Restore</strong> takes either one back: the server checks the file (SQLite, integrity, required tables, at least one admin), keeps a copy of the current pod in <code>data/before-restore-*.pod</code>, swaps it in, unpacks media (images and PDF only, real file type checked) and rewrites media paths for the new host. This is also how you move to another host. Limits: <code>max_restore_mb</code> and PHP's <code>upload_max_filesize</code> / <code>post_max_size</code>; put very large media folders on the new host via FTP. Backups contain password hashes, so keep them private.</p>

<h2>Sign in with Digi-ID</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Account → Digi-ID</strong> a user links a DigiByte address (the password is asked once). The login page then offers <strong>Sign in with Digi-ID</strong>: scan the QR code with a Digi-ID wallet and you are in, without a password. The wallet signs a one-time URI that contains your domain; it expires after five minutes and works once. The password login stays as the fallback. It needs a public HTTPS address and the PHP <code>gmp</code> or <code>bcmath</code> extension (with bcmath a sign-in takes about 0.3 s of server time). Legacy addresses starting with <code>D</code> are supported. <a href="/docs/security">Details →</a></p>

<h2>Rebuild on publish</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Settings → Build</strong>, enter a build webhook (Netlify, Vercel, GitHub Actions …). Lander calls it with a POST whenever an entry is published. Under <strong>Settings → Webhooks</strong> you can add signed hooks for publish and delete events: the body is JSON, the header <code>X-Orbiter-Signature: sha256=…</code> is the HMAC-SHA256 of <code>timestamp.body</code> with the hook's secret (shown once). The last 50 deliveries are logged. Each hook gets one attempt; there is no retry queue.</p>

<h2>Update</h2>
<p>When a newer release exists, the dashboard and <strong>Settings → Pod</strong> show it with an <strong>Update now</strong> button. Lander downloads the release ZIP and its signature from GitHub and checks the Ed25519 signature against the public key built into the code. Only then does it replace <code>src/</code>, <code>bin/</code> and <code>public/</code>; the config, <code>data/</code>, media and the subfolder paths stay. A copy of the pod and the previous code is saved to <code>data/update-rollback/</code> first.</p>
<p>It needs the PHP <code>zip</code> and <code>sodium</code> extensions and outgoing HTTPS. If something is missing, the admin tells you what, and you replace the files by hand: unzip the release over the install, keep <code>lander.config.php</code> and <code>data/</code>, and run <code>php bin/lander rebase &lt;base&gt;</code> in a subfolder. <code>update_check =&gt; false</code> turns the hint and the button off. Updating from 0.3.0 to 0.3.1 is manual (0.3.0 has no button); from there on it is one click.</p>

<h3>Where is the update button?</h3>
<p>The update line sits at the very bottom of the <strong>Pod</strong> card in <strong>Settings</strong>; the dashboard shows the same hint at the top. It always names the installed version. The <strong>Update now</strong> button appears <em>only if a newer release exists</em>. If you uploaded the files by hand and the line reads your version without a hint, you are up to date and there is nothing to click. Reload the admin with Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+R first, otherwise the browser may still show old files.</p>
<p>No update line at all means the install is older than 0.3.1. Update it once by hand (unzip over <code>src/</code>, <code>bin/</code>, <code>public/</code>; never replace <code>public/media/</code> or <code>data/</code>); from then on it is one click.</p>

<h2>Differences to Orbiter</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Orbiter</th><th>Lander</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Runtime</td><td>Node (Hono)</td><td>PHP 8.1+ (PDO SQLite)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Media</td><td>BLOB in the SQL file or S3</td><td>Files in a folder</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sections</td><td>all</td><td>Dashboard, collections, entries, editor, media, import, settings, account</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reviewer role, per-collection limits, review workflow, analytics, form builder</td><td>yes</td><td>not yet</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

